// Guided, in-app crash course from first principles through introductory college finance.
export const lessonCore={
first:[
['Start with claims on money','A financial asset is a claim: it may represent ownership, a promise to repay, or a pooled interest in other assets. A common stock is an ownership claim on a corporation. A bond is a loan claim with contractual terms. A bank deposit is a claim on the bank, subject to account terms and applicable protections. These claims have different rights and risks.','The word “investment” does not make an asset safe. A stock can lose most or all of its value; a bond issuer can default; a fund can decline; cash can lose purchasing power to inflation. Begin by asking: Who owes or owns what? What could make the claim worth less?'],
['Stocks: ownership and residual claims','When a company issues common stock, investors own shares representing a fractional interest. Shareholders may receive dividends if declared and may have voting rights, but they do not have a guaranteed payment schedule. In liquidation, creditors and preferred claims generally rank ahead of common shareholders.','A share price is the market price for a small ownership claim—not the company’s revenue, cash balance or guaranteed future value. If a company has 1 billion shares at $10 each, its equity market capitalization is $10 billion. That calculation describes market value of equity at that price, not the enterprise’s total value.'],
['Bonds: lending and repayment','A bond is a debt security. The issuer promises payments according to terms, often periodic interest and principal at maturity. A bond’s coupon rate is set in the contract; its yield reflects market price and expected payments. Credit risk is the chance of missed payment; interest-rate risk is the possibility that market yields move and the bond’s price changes.','If market rates rise, existing fixed-rate bonds usually become less attractive than new bonds, so their prices generally fall. Longer-maturity bonds tend to be more sensitive to rate changes, all else equal. A bond fund has no single maturity date for an investor in the same way an individual bond does, and its value can fluctuate.'],
['Funds: pooled ownership','A mutual fund or exchange-traded fund pools investor money to hold securities under a stated strategy. An index fund seeks to track a specified index; an active fund selects holdings according to its manager’s process. A fund gives exposure to a basket, but it is not automatically diversified: a narrow sector fund remains concentrated.','Read the fund’s objective, holdings, expense ratio, turnover, risks and prospectus. The expense ratio is an annual operating cost expressed as a percentage of assets; it reduces returns over time. ETFs trade during market hours at market prices that can differ slightly from net asset value; mutual fund shares typically transact at a calculated end-of-day net asset value.'],
['Index is a measuring rule; product is an investment','An index is a rule-based calculation tracking a selected group of securities. You generally cannot buy the index itself. A fund may seek to track it, but that fund has expenses, tracking difference and operational details. Two funds referencing the same index can differ in fees, tax behavior, trading and securities-lending practices.','For a market-cap-weighted index, larger companies have more influence. Equal-weighted indices give constituents more similar starting weights and rebalance accordingly. Index membership and weighting rules shape what the index represents. So ask not only “which index?” but “what holdings, methodology, costs and risks come with this investable product?”'],
['Worked comparison and knowledge check','Suppose you put a hypothetical $100 across one stock, one bond fund and cash. The stock and bond fund prices move; the cash amount is nominally stable but inflation may erode its purchasing power. The result depends on the assets and timeframe. This example shows why a label like “portfolio” is not enough to describe risk.','Before buying, identify the asset, the account holding it, its costs, how it can lose money, when you need the money and what evidence supports the investment case. This is general education, not a recommendation. A suitable choice depends on individual circumstances and can require qualified advice.']
],
risk:[
['Return is a change in value, not a promise','A simple holding-period return is (ending value − starting value + cash received) ÷ starting value. If a hypothetical share rises from $50 to $54 and pays $1, the return is $5 ÷ $50 = 10%. Real investment returns also depend on dates, fees, taxes, reinvestment and the exact measurement method.','A nominal return is not adjusted for inflation. A real return estimates purchasing-power change; approximately, real return ≈ nominal return − inflation for modest rates. Exact calculation is (1 + nominal return) ÷ (1 + inflation) − 1. Taxes and fees further reduce what an investor keeps.'],
['Risk has several dimensions','Volatility describes how much returns vary; it does not capture every risk. Permanent loss can result from a failing business or default. Liquidity risk means being unable to sell quickly at a fair price. Concentration risk comes from depending heavily on one company, sector or economic driver. Inflation, currency, interest-rate and behavioral risks also matter.','A temporary price decline may be emotionally difficult but is not identical to permanent impairment. Yet an investor forced to sell to pay a near-term bill can turn a temporary decline into a realized loss. That is why the time at which money is needed belongs in risk analysis.'],
['Time horizon and capacity','Time horizon is when funds are expected to be used. Risk capacity is the financial ability to withstand loss without derailing essential goals; risk tolerance is the emotional willingness to accept uncertainty. They can differ. A young investor can still have low capacity for risk with money needed for tuition next semester.','Separate goals by timing and flexibility. Near-term expenses often need liquidity and stability; long-term goals can allow more time for fluctuations, but no horizon guarantees recovery. Emergency needs, debt, income reliability, dependents and access to other resources all affect the analysis.'],
['Diversification and correlation','Diversification spreads exposure across securities, sectors, issuers, regions or asset classes. It reduces the impact that one holding can have if the other exposures do not move exactly together. Correlation measures how returns have moved in relation historically; it can change, especially during market stress. Diversification cannot remove broad market risk or ensure profit.','A portfolio holding 20 technology stocks may still be concentrated in one sector. A broad fund can spread company-specific exposure but remain mostly exposed to equity-market risk. Look through to holdings and economic drivers; count the actual risks, not just the number of fund tickers.'],
['Drawdowns, benchmarks and behavior','A drawdown is a decline from a prior peak to a later trough. A 20% decline requires a 25% gain from the lower value to return to the original amount: $80 × 1.25 = $100. Losses and gains are asymmetric in percentage terms.','A benchmark helps compare performance against a relevant market or strategy, but it is not a guarantee or an investable asset. Avoid comparing different time periods or risk levels. A written plan can reduce impulsive decisions; review it when goals or facts change, not only when headlines are alarming.'],
['A practical risk map','For each goal write: amount, date needed, flexibility, source of funds, and what a 10%, 25% or larger decline would mean. Those are stress scenarios, not predictions. Then list the risks of the proposed account and holdings separately. The account’s tax treatment does not remove investment risk.','There is no universal risk score or allocation that fits everyone. This course gives concepts for asking better questions, not a personalized portfolio. For a consequential personal decision, consider a qualified professional who understands your whole situation.']
],
compound:[
['Simple growth and contributions','If $1,000 earns a hypothetical 5% over one year, the gain is $50 and the ending balance is $1,050 before costs and taxes. If the next year also returns 5%, the gain applies to $1,050, not just the original $1,000. That reinvestment is compounding.','Regular contributions add new principal. The future value depends on the amount, timing, duration and sequence of returns. A calculator that assumes smooth annual returns is illustrating arithmetic, not predicting an investment path. Actual markets rise and fall.'],
['The compound-growth formula','For a one-time amount with a constant hypothetical annual rate, future value = present value × (1 + r)^n, where r is the annual rate and n is years. With periodic deposits, each deposit compounds for a different amount of time. The monthly calculator approximates these steps under its stated assumptions.','A percentage rate must be interpreted carefully: is it nominal or effective, annual or monthly, before or after fees? A 5% annual return does not mean 5% every month. The calculator excludes uncertain future variability unless it explicitly models scenarios.'],
['Why time and consistency matter','Longer time can magnify both gains and losses because returns compound. Starting earlier can increase the time contributions have to grow, but it does not remove risk. The size and consistency of contributions may matter more than trying to guess the best entry date.','An investor who contributes during declines buys more units for the same dollars, but those contributions can still lose value. A smooth chart can make growth look certain; the true path may include long flat periods and deep drawdowns.'],
['Inflation, costs and taxes','A nominal balance does not show what it can buy. If investments grow 5% and prices rise 3%, the approximate real return is about 2% before costs and taxes. Fees compound too: every dollar paid is a dollar no longer available to grow. Taxable distributions and sales may reduce the after-tax result.','Use realistic net-return assumptions and compare multiple scenarios rather than choosing a high number because it produces an attractive result. No assumed return in the lab is an expected outcome or a promise.'],
['Sequence and uncertainty','The order of returns matters when withdrawals occur. Two portfolios can have the same average return but different outcomes if losses arrive early while money is being withdrawn. This is sequence-of-returns risk. Contributions and withdrawals change the path, not merely the final average.','A prudent scenario exercise varies return, time, contribution and withdrawal assumptions one at a time, then tests a difficult sequence. Label unknowns clearly. A projection supports planning; it cannot tell you what markets will do.'],
['How to use the calculator','First note which inputs are under your control (such as planned contributions) and which are uncertain (returns, inflation, fees). Change only one at a time so you can see its effect. Compare nominal and inflation-adjusted results if available and note what the model excludes.','Do not make a real account decision from one number. Revisit assumptions and consider cash needs, taxes, costs and risk. Even a carefully modeled plan can differ from the future.']
],
fees:[
['Know the cost categories','Investing costs can include fund expense ratios, account service charges, transaction commissions, bid-ask spreads, advisory fees, transfer or closure fees and taxes. “Commission-free” trading does not mean an investment has no costs. A spread is the difference between the price someone will buy for and the price someone will sell for.','Read the provider’s fee schedule and fund prospectus. Check whether costs are one-time, recurring, account-level or embedded in a product. A cash sweep rate or margin interest rate also affects outcomes; borrowing through margin can increase losses and trigger forced sales.'],
['Percentage fees compound in reverse','A 1% annual fee on $10,000 is about $100 in the first year if the balance stays constant; the amount changes with the balance. Over time, fees also reduce the base that could have earned future returns. The effect is larger when the fee is high, the horizon long or the balance large.','Compare like with like: a service fee may pay for advice, planning or administration, while a fund expense ratio pays fund operating costs. Lower cost alone does not prove a product is better; compare what is delivered, conflicts, risks and alternatives.'],
['Trading costs and execution','A market order prioritizes execution, not a particular price. In a fast or illiquid market, the final price can differ from the last displayed quote. A limit order specifies the worst price you are willing to accept but may not execute. Order type, trading hours and liquidity affect execution.','The bid is what buyers are offering and the ask is what sellers are requesting. The spread is a transaction cost implicit in trading. Frequent trading can increase spreads, fees, taxes and behavioral errors even when the broker charges no explicit commission.'],
['Fund costs and tracking','An index fund can have an expense ratio, trading costs and tracking difference from its benchmark. A fund may use sampling, hold cash or experience timing differences. For ETFs, premium or discount to net asset value and bid-ask spread may matter, especially in less liquid markets.','Compare the exact share class and prospectus; similarly named funds can have different costs and holdings. Understand whether published performance includes reinvested dividends and fees. Past returns cannot establish that a low-cost fund will outperform or prevent loss.'],
['Compare providers with a checklist','Compare account fees, investment menu, minimums, service channels, cash treatment, order types, margin terms, transfer costs, tax documents, security features and how easy it is to find statements. Check official current disclosures because fee schedules and platform features change.','Estimate a realistic annual dollar cost for the account and investments. Separate certain fees from contingent costs. Ask what services you will actually use and whether a conflict could influence a recommendation.'],
['Worked comparison','Imagine two hypothetical funds with identical holdings and risk, one costing 0.05% and another 0.50% annually. The difference is 0.45 percentage points of assets each year before compounding and other costs. If their strategies or services differ, compare those too; the arithmetic alone does not determine suitability.','Practice translating percentages into dollars: fee rate × invested balance. Then ask how the fee is charged and whether other expenses apply. This simple check helps you understand disclosures before making any decision.']
],
statements:[
['The three statements answer different questions','The income statement measures revenue, expenses and profit over a period. The balance sheet lists assets, liabilities and equity at a point in time. The cash-flow statement organizes cash from operating, investing and financing activities. Together they describe performance, resources, obligations and cash movement.','Accrual accounting records revenue when earned and expenses when incurred under accounting rules, not necessarily when cash moves. Accounts receivable, inventory, accounts payable and deferred revenue explain why reported profit and cash flow differ.'],
['Income statement: profit is layered','Revenue minus cost of goods sold gives gross profit. Subtract operating expenses to reach operating income; interest and taxes lead toward net income. Labels and subtotals vary by company. Gross margin = gross profit ÷ revenue; operating margin = operating income ÷ revenue. Ratios need comparison over time and against relevant peers.','Growth in revenue may coexist with declining margins if input costs, discounting or investment rise faster. One-time items, stock compensation and acquisitions can complicate comparisons. Read notes and management discussion; do not assume adjusted metrics are standardized.'],
['Balance sheet: what is owned and owed','Assets include cash, receivables, inventory, property and intangible assets. Liabilities include payables, debt, leases and other obligations; equity is the residual claim under accounting rules. The balance sheet identity is assets = liabilities + equity. It is a snapshot, not a complete valuation of the company.','Liquidity ratios compare short-term resources with near-term obligations, but quality matters: slow inventory or doubtful receivables may be less useful than cash. Debt maturity, interest terms, covenants and off-balance-sheet commitments can alter financial flexibility.'],
['Cash-flow statement: follow the cash','Operating cash flow starts with net income and adjusts for noncash items and working-capital changes. Investing cash flow includes capital expenditures and acquisitions. Financing cash flow includes debt, equity and distributions. Free cash flow is a commonly used analytical measure, often operating cash flow minus capital expenditures, but definitions vary.','A company can report positive earnings while operating cash flow is weak because customers have not paid or inventory has grown. Capital spending may support future capacity, so a single year’s free cash flow must be interpreted in context.'],
['How the statements connect','Depreciation lowers accounting profit but is added back in operating cash flow because it is noncash in the current period. Capital expenditure uses cash and creates a long-lived asset that is depreciated over time. If inventory rises, cash may fall even when sales are growing.','These links help you test narratives. If a company claims strong demand, compare revenue, receivables, inventory and cash collection. If debt rises, look at financing cash flow, interest expense and maturity notes. Reconcile explanations with reported data.'],
['A repeatable company-reading workflow','Use the latest annual report for business description, risk factors, audited statements and notes; use quarterly reports to understand more recent changes. Compare several years and segment results. Track share count because dilution can reduce each share’s claim on total company value.','Write three observations, three questions and one uncertainty before forming a conclusion. Distinguish reported fact from your interpretation. Financial statements are essential evidence, but they do not by themselves determine what a security is worth.']
],
thesis:[
['Start with the business, not the ticker','Describe the product, customer, revenue model and reason customers choose the company. Identify where revenue comes from and what costs are required to deliver it. If you cannot explain how the business earns cash, you are not yet ready to estimate its value.','Use annual and quarterly filings, earnings releases and company presentations as starting evidence. Company materials are useful but are not neutral; compare claims with audited statements, customer evidence, competitors and risk disclosures.'],
['Build a driver-based forecast','Forecast important drivers rather than extending a single growth rate. A retailer may depend on store count, comparable sales and gross margin; a software firm may depend on customer additions, retention, pricing and cloud costs. Connect operational assumptions to revenue, expenses, investment and cash.','Use a base case and at least one alternative. Identify which assumptions have the greatest impact. A spreadsheet can create false precision; use ranges where evidence is weak and document the data source, date and logic for each important input.'],
['Valuation is conditional','A valuation estimates value given assumptions about future cash flows, growth, risk and capital structure. Discounted cash flow, comparable-company multiples and other methods each have limits. A model output is not an objective intrinsic truth. Sensitivity analysis reveals how much the answer changes when assumptions move.','Compare the valuation with the current market price while remembering the price reflects expectations and competing views. A low multiple can signal a bargain or a deteriorating business. A strong business can be overpriced. Avoid declaring “cheap” without explaining the risks and assumptions.'],
['Make the thesis falsifiable','A useful thesis states what you believe, why evidence supports it, what the market may be missing, what risks matter and what would change your mind. Include disconfirming evidence, not only confirmation. This protects against motivated reasoning and attachment to a prior opinion.','A catalyst is a possible event that reveals information or changes expectations; it is not necessarily a positive event or a guaranteed price move. Separate the operating thesis from a near-term price prediction. State your confidence and uncertainty clearly.'],
['Risk, sizing and suitability are distinct','Even a well-researched company can have volatile shares, high valuation risk, liquidity risk or an adverse event. Diversification can reduce dependence on one company but cannot guarantee gains. Position size changes the portfolio impact of being wrong; conviction alone does not erase risk.','A stock research exercise is not an instruction to trade. Personal goals, account type, taxes, time horizon and loss capacity matter. Do not invest money needed for near-term essentials based only on a persuasive thesis.'],
['Write and review the note','A concise note can follow: business model; evidence; forecast drivers; valuation range; bull/base/bear cases; risks; disconfirming evidence; and next review trigger. Cite sources and date the work. When new information arrives, identify what changed and update assumptions rather than merely adjusting the target.','Practice on a company without placing a trade. This builds analytical skill and keeps research separate from action. A forecast can be wrong even when the process is disciplined; evaluate reasoning and risk controls as well as the outcome.']
],
budget:[
['Map money in and money out','Start with reliable take-home income and support, then list fixed essentials, variable essentials, debt payments, flexible spending, savings and irregular costs. Use actual account or bill records where available; label estimates. A cash-flow plan is about timing as well as monthly totals.','Convert annual costs such as books, travel or insurance into a monthly set-aside. If tuition is due before income arrives, a monthly budget alone may hide the shortfall; draw a simple calendar of inflows and due dates.'],
['Distinguish needs, goals and constraints','A budget is a decision tool, not a judgment. Some costs are contractually fixed, while others are adjustable; categories depend on a person’s situation. Include minimum debt payments and required expenses before discretionary goals. Identify obligations, dependents and irregular income that affect flexibility.','If planned spending exceeds resources, test several levers: timing, discretionary costs, income, aid, payment plans or support. Do not assume all costs can be cut without consequence. Prioritize safety and essential needs.'],
['Build a buffer and handle debt thoughtfully','Accessible emergency savings can reduce the need to borrow for an unexpected bill. A useful target depends on expenses, income stability, insurance and support; start with an attainable amount and build over time. Keep short-term cash needs separate from investments that can fluctuate.','Debt has interest, fees and contractual consequences. List balance, rate, minimum, due date and terms. High-cost debt can compound against you. Compare repayment strategies while maintaining liquidity for foreseeable bills; there is no one-size answer for every household.'],
['Track and revise','At the end of a week or month, compare plan with actuals. Ask whether a difference reflects timing, an incorrect estimate, a one-time event or a recurring pattern. Adjust future assumptions without treating a single unusual month as permanent.','A budget can be simple: spreadsheet, notes or a secure bank tool. Avoid sharing account credentials with an app. Protect sensitive information. This practice calculator’s entries are not saved or transmitted.'],
['Example: textbook month','Suppose a student receives $1,600 monthly and spends $1,150 on recurring essentials, $200 on flexible costs, $100 on debt and $100 on savings. The apparent remainder is $50. If books cost $300 once each semester, reserving roughly $50 monthly for six months uses that entire remainder.','The point is not that these numbers fit you; they are fictional. Irregular expenses can make an apparently balanced plan fragile. Use actual dates, costs and income, then select a realistic adjustment if the monthly total does not balance.'],
['Budget as a feedback loop','A useful cycle is plan, record, compare, explain and revise. Set a small number of categories that help decisions. Make room for goals and enjoyment, and revisit the plan after changes in school, housing, employment or family circumstances.','Financial well-being is not measured by one budget percentage. Seek campus financial aid, student support or qualified nonprofit counseling when needed; verify that any service is reputable and appropriate.']
],
account:[
['Account versus investment','An account is the legal and tax container where assets are held; an investment is the security or cash position inside it. A brokerage account might hold stocks, bonds, mutual funds, ETFs or uninvested cash. An IRA is a retirement-account type, not a particular investment. Opening or depositing money does not automatically buy a security.','First determine the goal, timeline and access needs. Then compare account types and tax rules. Only after that compare investments and costs. This order prevents confusing a provider’s account label with a portfolio strategy.'],
['Taxable brokerage basics','A taxable brokerage account generally provides flexible access without IRA-style annual contribution limits. Dividends, interest and realized gains may have tax effects. Cash-versus-margin choices matter: margin is borrowing secured by investments and can magnify losses or lead to forced sales. A cash account avoids borrowing to buy securities but still has market and settlement risks.','Review fee schedule, eligible securities, account protections, cash sweep terms, tax forms, transfer rules and order mechanics. SIPC protection has limits and does not protect against investment losses or guarantee a security’s value; check current official materials.'],
['Retirement accounts and employer plans','A 401(k) is an employer-sponsored plan with rules, investment options and possible employer contributions defined by the plan. Read the Summary Plan Description and understand eligibility, matching formula, vesting, fees and withdrawal restrictions. A match is part of compensation, but personal cash needs and plan terms still matter.','IRAs are opened individually. Roth and traditional IRAs have contribution eligibility, annual limits and distribution rules. Traditional contributions may be deductible depending on circumstances; Roth contributions are generally after-tax, and qualified distributions can be tax-free when requirements are met. Tax results are not universal.'],
['Opening sequence and security','Compare provider cost, investment choice, service, cash treatment, account minimums, transfer process and security features. Verify the provider’s official website address before entering private information. Providers may request identity, address, employment and other information to meet legal requirements. Finance Campus never collects those details.','Complete the application directly with the provider, review disclosures and beneficiaries, link funding securely and understand the tax year for IRA contributions. Account approval does not mean the account is invested. Beware of unsolicited links and never share one-time passcodes.'],
['Funding, settlement and orders','A transfer may take time to clear. Brokerage cash may be held in a settlement fund, core position or sweep program under provider terms. To invest, select a security and understand order type, trading hours, bid-ask spread, costs and risk. A market order seeks execution, not a guaranteed price; a limit order may not fill.','After submitting an order, check confirmation, quantity, price and status. Keep records. Understand recurring-investment settings, cancellation timing and how dividends are handled. These are mechanical concepts, not instructions to buy a specific investment.'],
['An account-opening decision tree','If the goal is general flexible investing, study taxable brokerage rules. If it is retirement, compare employer plan access with IRA options, eligibility and tax treatment. For education savings, learn 529 rules and state treatment. For each account, verify the applicable year’s official rules before acting.','This course gives a process, not individualized tax advice. Contribution limits, income rules and tax treatment can change. Use the current IRS materials and provider disclosures; consider a qualified professional for decisions that depend on your personal facts.']
],
ira:[
['Traditional IRA: tax timing','A traditional IRA may allow deductible contributions, depending on income, filing status and workplace-plan coverage; distributions are generally taxable under applicable rules. Nondeductible contributions require records and may affect later tax calculations. Do not assume every contribution is deductible or every withdrawal is taxed identically.','The account is subject to contribution eligibility, annual limits and distribution rules. Early distributions may trigger taxes or additional tax unless an exception applies. Required distribution rules can apply later in life. Verify the current rule and your circumstances with IRS materials or a qualified tax professional.'],
['Roth IRA: contributions and qualified distributions','Roth IRA contributions are generally made with after-tax dollars and are not deductible. Qualified distributions can be tax-free if applicable holding-period and qualifying-event requirements are satisfied. Eligibility to contribute can phase out with income; the annual limit and earned compensation rules also apply.','Roth accounts can have ordering rules and special treatment for contributions, conversions and earnings. “I can withdraw it anytime” oversimplifies the rules. Understand the specific transaction and tax year before acting; a withdrawal may reduce future tax-advantaged growth or create tax consequences.'],
['Annual contribution limit and eligibility','For 2026, the general IRA contribution limit is $7,500, with a $1,100 catch-up contribution for those age 50 or older, subject to IRS rules. Roth and traditional IRA contributions share the same combined limit. Contributions are also generally constrained by eligible compensation; a spousal IRA has specific requirements.','Roth income eligibility and the deductibility of traditional contributions depend on facts such as filing status, modified adjusted gross income and workplace-plan coverage. Exact thresholds and rules change by tax year. Confirm with the IRS instead of relying on a prior year’s number or a social-media summary.'],
['Roth, traditional or taxable: frame the tradeoff','Roth contributions pay tax now under current rules; qualified distributions may be tax-free later. Traditional contributions may produce a current deduction, with taxable distributions later. Taxable accounts offer flexibility but may create current tax on income or gains. These broad descriptions omit personal tax rates, state rules and future law.','Compare liquidity needs, eligibility, current and possible future tax circumstances, employer-plan access, costs and withdrawal rules. Nobody can know future tax rates with certainty. There is no universal account that is best for every student. A tax professional can assess details that this lesson cannot.'],
['Choose investments separately','An IRA can hold different types of eligible investments depending on its provider. The account itself does not produce a return. If cash remains in the settlement position, it may earn interest under provider terms but may not meet the intended investment plan. Understand available funds, expense ratios and risk.','Portfolio decisions should reflect goals and uncertainty, not just the account label. Diversification can reduce company-specific risk but does not prevent loss. Never select an investment only because it appears in the opening flow or is a default option; read what it holds and costs.'],
['A safe verification workflow','Before contributing, confirm current-year eligibility, combined remaining limit, compensation, income thresholds, account fees and funding year. Save confirmations and tax records. For rollovers, conversions or distributions, learn the specific tax treatment before moving money; these events can be complex.','Use official IRS publications for tax rules and provider disclosures for operational steps. The provider’s screen may change and cannot determine every individual tax consequence. Ask a qualified tax adviser when your circumstances make the answer unclear.']
],
macro:[
['What an index is—and is not','A market index applies rules to a group of securities and combines their prices or returns into a measure. The S&P 500 broadly represents U.S. large-cap equities and uses float-adjusted market-cap weighting; the Dow is a price-weighted set of 30 companies; Nasdaq-100 tracks large Nasdaq-listed nonfinancial companies. Each captures a different slice and method.','An index is not the whole economy and generally is not directly investable. A fund tracking the index is a separate product with fees, tracking difference and trading details. Knowing the index methodology helps you understand what a live line actually represents.'],
['Read the chart axes and date range','The horizontal axis shows time; the vertical axis shows a quote or index level, depending on the selected instrument. A plotted point is tied to a time interval—for example, one day of data with a daily interval or a year of data with a daily interval. Hovering may reveal a date, value and change.','The widget can show a percentage change, point change or both. Points are index-level units, not dollars invested; percent change allows comparison across different starting levels. A truncated vertical axis can visually amplify a small movement, so inspect labels and scale before judging its size.'],
['Choose the time window and interval','A one-day view emphasizes intraday movement and may be noisy. A multi-month or multi-year view helps reveal broader patterns but hides short-term swings. Interval controls determine how often observations are plotted. A daily bar summarizes a day; a weekly bar summarizes a week. Market closures and time zones can create gaps.','Always state both window and interval when describing a chart. “Up” without a period is incomplete. Compare the same dates and return definition. This dashboard offers several date ranges; settings from the chart provider can change and data can be delayed.'],
['Understand the line and index composition','A line or filled area chart connects values over time; the shaded area is visual styling, not an additional quantity. A rising line means the quoted index level increased over that period; it does not show that every constituent rose. Weighting determines how much each member contributes. In cap-weighted indices, the largest firms have more influence.','Market breadth asks how many securities participated in the move. An index can rise while many constituents fall if a few heavily weighted firms rise enough. Compare equal-weight measures, sector performance or advance/decline data to study participation, while remembering each measure has limitations.'],
['Price return, total return and dividends','A price index reflects security prices under its calculation rules. A total-return version incorporates reinvested dividends. Over time, these can diverge because dividends contribute to investor return. The chart widget’s displayed symbol and provider configuration determine which series you are viewing; do not assume dividend reinvestment is included.','An ETF proxy such as SPY, QQQ or IWM is a traded fund, not the underlying index. Its market price, fund expenses, distributions and tracking behavior can differ from the index. This app’s graph labels index and ETF symbols so you can tell the concepts apart.'],
['How to read a move without overreacting','First describe the observation: index, date range, percent or points, and magnitude. Then identify the data source and timestamp. Next compare with a relevant baseline, such as the prior period or another index. Finally ask what could explain it and what evidence would distinguish explanations. One chart alone cannot establish cause.','Interest rates, inflation, earnings expectations, currency, positioning and company-specific news can interact. A headline may coincide with a move without causing it. Separate observed facts from interpretation, and avoid treating a market snapshot as a forecast or a personal investment signal.'],
['A worked chart-reading checklist','Example sentence structure: “The Nasdaq-100 proxy is up/down by X% over the selected window, measured at Y interval; the provider reports time Z. This measure is concentrated in large nonfinancial Nasdaq-listed firms, so it is not a complete picture of every U.S. stock.” Fill in values from the live graph; do not invent missing data.','Before sharing an observation, record instrument, period, units, time and source. Then ask about breadth, index weighting, dividends and the macro context. This is a repeatable way to read a chart critically, not a method for predicting the next move.']
],
diversify:[
['Diversification starts with exposure','A portfolio’s risk depends on how much each holding contributes and how holdings move together. Owning many securities can reduce exposure to one issuer, but a group of similar companies may share the same economic driver. Identify sector, region, currency, size and factor concentrations.','Market-cap-weighted funds can have large positions in their biggest constituents. Two funds may overlap substantially. Review top holdings and portfolio exposure rather than counting tickers or fund names.'],
['Correlation and covariance','Covariance measures how two variables move together in absolute units; correlation standardizes covariance between −1 and +1. A low or negative historical correlation may reduce portfolio variation in that sample, but it is not fixed. Correlations can rise during crises or shift after structural changes.','Portfolio variance includes both each asset’s variance and cross terms reflecting covariance. This explains why a portfolio can be less volatile than the weighted average of its constituents, but only when the relationships support it. Estimates are uncertain and sensitive to the observation window.'],
['Asset classes and distinct risks','Equities carry business and valuation risk; bonds face credit, interest-rate and inflation risk; cash has reinvestment and purchasing-power risk; real assets and commodities add their own cycles and liquidity considerations. Labels do not guarantee diversification if underlying drivers overlap.','International exposure can add different industries and currencies, but it introduces political, regulatory, tax and currency risks. Small-company securities may behave differently from large ones but can be less liquid and more volatile. Diversification is not simply “add more asset classes.”'],
['Allocation and rebalancing','Asset allocation is the portfolio’s broad distribution among asset categories. It should be tied to the goal and constraints. Rebalancing returns weights toward chosen targets through purchases, sales or new contributions. It may limit drift but can create transaction costs or taxable gains.','A rebalance rule can be calendar-based or threshold-based; each has tradeoffs. Do not confuse rebalancing with market timing or assume it raises returns. The targets themselves may be wrong for a particular investor, and rebalancing cannot prevent loss.'],
['Risk budgeting and scenarios','Risk budgeting asks how much risk each exposure contributes to the whole portfolio. A smaller weight in a highly volatile holding can still dominate risk. Stress scenarios explore how a portfolio might respond to rate increases, recession, currency moves or liquidity stress. Scenarios are illustrations, not predictions.','Consider both portfolio drawdown and the investor’s capacity to wait. Money needed soon should not automatically bear the same risk as a distant goal. Evaluate emergency reserves and debt separately from long-term investing.'],
['A diversified portfolio is still uncertain','Diversification reduces some concentration risks but does not remove systemic market risk, inflation, fraud, bad assumptions or the possibility of correlated losses. It does not ensure profit or protect principal. A broad index can decline significantly.','A meaningful review asks what the portfolio holds, what each position contributes, why it belongs, how it could fail and when the goal needs the funds. This lesson teaches analysis, not a recommended allocation.']
]};
Object.assign(lessonCore,{
statements:[
['The accounting equation','The balance sheet is built around assets = liabilities + equity. Assets are resources controlled by an organization; liabilities are obligations; equity is the residual claim. Every recorded transaction affects at least two accounts under double-entry bookkeeping. The equation balances, but it does not mean the company is economically healthy.','Accrual accounting recognizes economic activity when earned or incurred under rules, which can precede cash. Revenue recognition, estimates and classifications involve judgment. Read audited statements and footnotes to understand what the numbers include.'],
['Income statement and margins','Revenue is the amount recognized from sales and services. Subtract cost of revenue to get gross profit; subtract operating expenses to reach operating income; subtract financing costs and taxes to reach net income, with company-specific details. Margins divide a profit measure by revenue and show how much remains per dollar of sales.','A rising margin may reflect pricing power, lower costs, product mix or accounting effects. A falling margin may reflect input inflation or investment. Compare time periods, peers and business segments; a single year or headline earnings-per-share figure can hide important changes.'],
['Balance sheet and working capital','Current assets and liabilities are expected to be realized or settled within the operating cycle or year under accounting conventions. Receivables are amounts customers owe; inventory is goods held for sale; payables are amounts owed to suppliers. These accounts connect operations to cash.','Working capital changes can consume or release cash. Growing receivables faster than revenue may prompt questions about collection or terms; excess inventory can become obsolete. These are investigation signals, not proof of misconduct. Read company explanations and compare several periods.'],
['Cash flows: operating, investing, financing','Operating cash flow adjusts earnings for noncash items and working capital. Investing cash flow includes capital purchases and acquisitions. Financing cash flow includes borrowing, repayment, share issuance, repurchases and distributions. The categories describe different economic choices and should be read together.','Capital expenditure can reduce current cash but support future output. Free cash flow is often approximated as operating cash flow less capital expenditure, but analysts define it differently. State the formula used and do not compare inconsistent measures.'],
['Ratios are questions, not answers','Liquidity ratios assess near-term obligations; leverage ratios compare debt with earnings, assets or equity; profitability ratios measure margins or returns on capital; valuation multiples compare price or enterprise value with a financial measure. Ratios help organize comparison, but accounting, sector and business model differences matter.','A high return on equity can reflect strong operations, leverage, a small equity base or buybacks. A low price-to-earnings ratio can indicate low expectations or real distress. Ask which economic mechanism generated the ratio and whether it can persist.'],
['Read the filing and triangulate','Read the annual report’s business section, risk factors, management discussion, statements, segment disclosures and notes. Check how revenue is recognized, stock compensation, debt maturity, leases, litigation and share-count changes. Compare the latest quarter with prior periods and management explanations.','Triangulate company statements with cash flows, competitors and industry data. Separate reported facts from management estimates and your own conclusions. The filings describe past performance and risks; they do not guarantee future results or reveal a single certain valuation.']
],
thesis:[
['Investment research is a chain of reasoning','A thesis is a testable explanation linking the business, future economics and today’s price. Start with what the company sells, who pays and why. Then identify the drivers of revenue, margins, investment and cash. Research is stronger when every major claim can be traced to evidence.','Keep a source log with document, date and page or table. Separate fact (“reported revenue grew 8%”) from interpretation (“retention may be improving”) and forecast (“I estimate growth of 6%”). This prevents an assumption from quietly becoming a fact.'],
['Understand the industry and advantage','Study customers, substitutes, suppliers, competitors, regulation and barriers to entry. An advantage might come from scale, brand, network effects, switching costs, patents or distribution, but each can erode. Ask what evidence shows customers stay or competitors struggle.','A popular product is not automatically a durable economic moat. Look for pricing, retention, cost advantage and returns on invested capital over time. Consider how a competitor, technology shift or policy change could weaken the advantage.'],
['Turn drivers into forecasts','Forecast by operational drivers: customers × usage × price, units × average selling price, stores × comparable sales, or another metric suited to the business. Then estimate costs, taxes, working capital and investment needed to support growth. Forecasting is conditional; state assumptions rather than presenting guesses as facts.','Build base, upside and downside scenarios. Test which assumptions matter most and whether they are supported by historical evidence or external constraints. Include share count and capital structure when thinking per share; growth in the business does not always mean growth in each share’s value.'],
['Valuation, expectations and uncertainty','A discounted cash-flow model discounts forecast cash flows for time and risk. Comparable multiples use market prices of similar firms. Both rely on assumptions and can produce a range. Compare current price with scenarios and identify what growth, margin or risk assumptions appear embedded.','Sensitivity analysis changes one or more inputs to expose fragility. A precise output such as $42.17 can imply unjustified certainty. Report a range, explain the largest drivers and identify evidence that would make the model obsolete.'],
['Risk and falsification','List specific risks, their plausible impact and indicators to monitor. Include company execution, competition, regulation, balance-sheet pressure, dilution, governance and valuation. A risk is not addressed by writing “the market may go down”; specify the exposure and mechanism.','Write down evidence that would weaken the thesis before you become attached. Set a review trigger, such as a change in customer retention or leverage. Updating a view is a strength when evidence changes; defending an old opinion regardless of facts is not.'],
['Communicate responsibly','A useful memo gives the business overview, thesis, evidence, forecast assumptions, valuation, risks, scenarios and sources. Explain uncertainty and avoid guaranteed language. Never use material nonpublic information or share it improperly. Do not treat a research exercise or target price as a personalized instruction to trade.','Research is a process, not a prediction contest. Review whether the reasoning was sound, assumptions were reasonable and risks were managed. A favorable outcome can come from luck; an unfavorable outcome can happen despite disciplined work.']
],
budget:[
['Why cash flow matters','Cash flow is the timing of money received and spent. A monthly surplus does not guarantee bills can be paid on the due date if income arrives later. Make a calendar of tuition, rent, aid disbursement, paychecks and irregular costs. Use after-tax take-home income for a household budget.','Separate confirmed amounts from estimates. If income is irregular, use a conservative baseline and note seasonal changes. Do not count a possible bonus, refund or future job as guaranteed cash.'],
['Make useful categories','Begin with housing, food, transport, tuition, health, communications, debt payments, savings and flexible spending, then adjust to fit reality. Fixed versus flexible is a planning distinction, not a moral label. Some “optional” costs protect health, learning or family responsibilities.','Use enough detail to make decisions but not so many categories that tracking becomes burdensome. Keep an irregular-expense fund for books, travel, annual subscriptions and repairs. Review a few months to discover patterns.'],
['Debt and interest','Interest is the cost of borrowing. With revolving credit, unpaid balances can accrue interest under the card agreement; paying only the minimum may extend repayment. Compare APR, fees, due date, promotional expiration and consequences of missed payments. Student loans have different federal or private terms and protections.','List balances and rates, then understand each contract. Paying expensive debt can avoid future interest, but draining all cash can create new borrowing after an emergency. Consider liquidity and required payments together; this course cannot choose for an individual.'],
['Savings, emergencies and goals','Savings reserves money for a purpose and timeframe. An emergency reserve is accessible for unexpected costs; a planned short-term expense is better tracked separately. Savings accounts have provider terms and variable rates; deposit insurance applies only to eligible deposits at covered institutions within limits and conditions.','Investing money needed soon can expose a goal to market losses at a bad time. Match liquidity and risk with when the funds are needed. Start with a specific achievable goal and revisit it as circumstances change.'],
['Use a zero-based or flexible plan','A zero-based plan assigns each expected dollar to a category, including savings, until planned inflows and outflows reconcile. A flexible plan sets guardrails for key categories and reviews actual spending. Neither method guarantees success; choose one that is understandable and sustainable.','When the numbers do not balance, compare income timing, aid, housing, discretionary items and payment options. Seek campus financial-aid or student-support resources if essential needs are at risk. Beware of predatory loans and fees.'],
['Monthly review and example','After a month, explain large differences: price, quantity, timing or a one-time event. Revise future assumptions, not the historical record. A budget is a feedback loop, not a promise that every month will be identical.','Example: a $240 annual book cost can be planned as about $20 per month if known in advance. If it arrives next month and no reserve exists, timing must be handled through available cash or another safe plan. The example is arithmetic, not a target.']
],
account:[
['Map the account universe','Taxable brokerage accounts offer flexible investing access and potential current tax on income or realized gains. Retirement accounts offer tax rules and access restrictions. Workplace plans such as 401(k)s have sponsor-specific terms; IRAs are individual accounts. Education savings plans such as 529s have qualified-use rules and possible state tax effects.','Accounts hold assets; the provider administers the account; the investment has its own risks and costs. A “cash” balance, a bank deposit and a money-market mutual fund are not interchangeable. Know what legal instrument holds your money and what protections apply.'],
['Custody, protection and cash sweep','A brokerage firm holds and processes securities under custody rules. SIPC may protect customer property if a member brokerage fails and property is missing, within limits and eligibility; it does not insure market value or protect against investment losses. Bank deposit insurance is separate and applies to eligible deposits under rules.','Brokerage cash may be swept to partner banks or held in a money-market fund. Those structures have different risks, insurance and liquidity terms. Read the current account agreement and cash-sweep disclosures rather than inferring protection from the word “cash.”'],
['Compare before opening','Compare account type, fees, available investments, account minimums, service, cash yield and sweep arrangement, transfer procedures, security tools and tax documents. For a retirement account, check eligibility, current-year contribution limits and withdrawal rules. An employer plan may include matching and vesting terms.','Verify the official domain and contact information. Do not enter personal identity data after following unsolicited links or ads. Brokers must verify identity; provide sensitive details only on the firm’s secure application. This learning app never requests that information.'],
['Application, funding and records','Application steps generally include selecting an account, providing identity and address details, answering required questions, reviewing agreements and submitting for verification. Add a beneficiary where relevant. Processing may take time. Avoid sharing passwords, security codes or account numbers with Finance Campus.','When funding an IRA, check contribution year and remaining eligible limit. For transfers and rollovers, different rules can apply. Keep confirmations, tax forms and statements securely. Mistakes with tax-year designation or transfers can be expensive to correct.'],
['How the investment order works','Once funds are available, select an investment and understand ticker, share class, amount, order type, quote timestamp and fees. Market orders can execute at a different price from a stale quote; limit orders constrain price but may not execute. Fractional-share rules vary by provider.','Review the confirmation and account holdings after placing any order. Cash can remain uninvested after a deposit. Dividends can be paid as cash or reinvested depending on settings. This is an explanation of mechanics, not a direction to transact.'],
['Protect the account and avoid scams','Use a unique password, multifactor authentication, account alerts and trusted devices. Verify unexpected requests through a known official channel. Be suspicious of guaranteed returns, urgent transfers, fake support numbers and requests for one-time passcodes. Review statements and report unauthorized activity promptly to the provider.','Before applying, check regulator resources for broker registration and disclosures. Account security is part of financial planning. Never paste keys or account credentials into AI chat; the app does not need them.']
],
ira:[
['Compare account types before tax labels','A Roth IRA, traditional IRA and taxable brokerage account differ in contribution rules, taxation, liquidity and reporting. Roth and traditional IRAs are both retirement accounts and share an annual contribution limit. Brokerage accounts have different tax treatment and generally more flexible access.','The investment chosen inside the account is a separate decision. A Roth IRA holding a risky asset can lose value; a cash account can earn little or lose purchasing power. Tax advantages do not guarantee an investment result.'],
['Roth IRA mechanics','Roth contributions are generally made with after-tax dollars and are not deductible. Contribution eligibility depends on compensation and income rules. Qualified distributions of earnings can be tax-free if applicable requirements are met. Ordering rules distinguish contributions, conversions and earnings.','Avoid simplifying this to “withdrawals are always tax-free.” Early distribution, conversion and five-year rules can matter. A withdrawal can also reduce future growth opportunity. Check the precise rule for the type of money and transaction before acting.'],
['Traditional IRA mechanics','Traditional IRA contributions may be deductible depending on filing status, income and workplace-plan coverage. Deductible contributions and earnings are generally taxed when distributed. Nondeductible contributions have different basis and reporting considerations. Required minimum distribution rules can apply later in life.','There may be additional tax on some early distributions, subject to exceptions. A rollover, conversion or distribution can produce complex tax effects. A provider’s app may show a button without explaining the user’s whole tax situation; verify IRS guidance or consult a tax professional.'],
['2026 limits and income checks','For tax year 2026, the general combined IRA contribution limit is $7,500, with a $1,100 age-50 catch-up contribution, subject to eligible compensation and other IRS rules. Roth and traditional IRA contributions count toward the same limit. Income phaseouts and deduction rules are separate tests.','Verify the tax year before contributing. An excess contribution can require correction and may trigger tax consequences. The limits, thresholds and rules can change; use official IRS information for the relevant year, not an old screenshot or a generic calculator.'],
['Employer plans and coordination','A 401(k) is governed by its plan document, investment menu and administrator. Employer match, vesting and fees can matter. An IRA can complement a workplace plan but does not replace its rules. Coordinating accounts means seeing overall allocation, fees and beneficiary information across providers.','Compare account access, investment options, portability, tax treatment and current cash needs. Employer plan assets and IRA assets have different protections and rollover rules. Do not move funds simply because an advertisement suggests consolidation.'],
['Decision process without a one-size answer','List current and future tax uncertainty, eligibility, time horizon, liquidity, employer contributions, fees and withdrawal rules. Compare scenarios with assumptions and note which facts are unknown. A student’s income or age alone does not determine the right account.','This course is general education, not individualized tax or investment advice. If deciding between Roth and traditional contributions affects your taxes, ask a qualified tax professional who can review your full facts. Confirm every limit with current IRS materials.']
],
macro:[
['Economic data versus market prices','Economic data describe activity under definitions and periods: inflation measures price changes, employment data estimate labor-market conditions, GDP measures output. Releases can be revised. Market prices reflect expectations about future cash flows, rates and risk as well as current facts.','A positive data release can coincide with falling prices if investors expected a stronger result or rates change. Correlation in time does not prove the report caused the move. First establish the fact, then examine plausible mechanisms and alternatives.'],
['Interest rates and valuation intuition','A bond yield is a return measure based on price and promised payments under assumptions. When market yields rise, existing fixed-rate bond prices generally fall. For equities, higher discount rates can reduce the present value of distant future cash flows, while borrowing costs can affect profits. Effects vary by duration, balance sheet and expectations.','A central bank policy rate is not the same as every mortgage, corporate borrowing or bond rate. Credit spread and maturity also matter. Read the specific rate series and source before describing “rates” as if there were only one.'],
['Inflation and company economics','Inflation is a rate of change in a price index, not the price level itself. A lower inflation rate means prices are rising more slowly, not necessarily falling. Companies may face higher wages, materials and financing costs while trying to pass costs to customers.','Pricing power and contract timing differ. Nominal revenue can rise while real unit sales fall. Use volume, price, margins and cash flows to understand what is happening rather than relying on the total-dollar headline.'],
['Earnings expectations and market reaction','An earnings report contains actual results, guidance, management commentary and sometimes analyst expectations. A share price can move based on the gap between result and expectations, forward guidance, margins, cash flow or risk. Consensus estimates are not a guarantee or necessarily a complete description of expectations.','Compare the reported period, prior-year and prior-quarter figures, guidance changes and revisions. After-hours prices can be less liquid and may change at the next session. A headline such as “earnings beat” compresses many details into one phrase.'],
['Index headlines and market breadth','A broad index summarizes a defined set of securities using a weighting formula. A cap-weighted index may be driven by its largest members; a price-weighted index such as the Dow reacts differently to constituent prices. Breadth measures participation, such as the share of constituents rising, but has multiple definitions.','Index gain does not mean every stock or household gained. Compare sector, equal-weighted and small-cap measures when asking whether a move is broad. Ensure the comparison uses compatible periods and return definitions.'],
['A disciplined news workflow','Record source, date, release period and exact statistic. Separate observation from interpretation. Identify the transmission channel—sales, costs, rates, financing or risk premium—and the companies or groups affected. Then name an alternative explanation and what further data would help distinguish it.','Avoid making trades from a headline or a single chart. Markets can be volatile, data revised and forecasts wrong. A market-pulse page is a teaching and observation tool, not investment advice or a prediction service.']
]});
